{"id": "swebq0216", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 2.74, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1307, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Sino-Tibetan languages", "query": "what was the ancient chinese writing system?", "transcript": "What was the ancient Chinese writing system?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The ancient Chinese writing system was called Chinese characters or Hanzi. These characters have been used for thousands of years to write the Chinese language. Unlike alphabetic systems where letters represent sounds, Chinese characters are logographic, meaning each character typically represents a word or a meaningful part of a word. The earliest known examples of Chinese characters come from oracle bones used in divination during the Shang Dynasty around 1200 BCE. Over time, characters evolved and were standardized, especially during the Qin Dynasty when Emperor Qin Shi Huang unified the writing system across China. Today, Chinese characters remain a vital part of written Chinese, with thousands of distinct characters used depending on the context.", "answer_ms": 3246}
